                  snippet:
                    "Multiple studies show remote employees often outperform expectations. However, this productivity boost depends on the intentional management practices—like clear goals, regular check-ins, and supportive technology.\nWith this regained time, employees set new routines and habits, often establishing more organized and dedicated workspaces at home to support productivity and work-life balance. This extra time is often reinvested into rest, personal projects, or family—key drivers of happiness and job satisfaction.\nMultiple studies show remote employees often outperform expectations. However, this productivity boost depends on the intentional management practices—like clear goals, regular check-ins, and supportive technology.\nThe rise of remote work has reshaped (and is continuing to reshape) employee expectations. In 2025, remote employee retention has become a key challenge and focus for organizations aiming to maintain a productive and engaged workforce.\nThe cost of high turnover in a remote-first world: Turnover costs can soar to more than 200% of an employee’s annual salary, considering recruitment, training, and lost productivity. Flexible work arrangements, like remote work, have been shown to significantly reduce turnover.",
